    <div style="background:#10233d;padding:20px;border-radius:14px;margin-bottom:20px;border:1px solid #33415c;border-left:4px solid #22d3ee;">
    <h3 style="color:#c8e6c9;margin-top:0;">🔭 Business Background</h3>
    <p><strong>Cutting:</strong> cut forage at <em>early flowering</em>, when it has the most leaves and nutrients; older forage becomes stemmy and less nutritious. Cut in the morning after the dew has dried, using a panga, slasher or sickle. <strong>Drying:</strong> spread the forage thinly in the sun; turn it two or three times a day with a fork or rake so it dries evenly. Drying usually takes two to four sunny days. Cover or heap it if rain threatens. <strong>Testing:</strong> hay is ready when stems snap rather than bend and no moisture appears when a handful is twisted. <strong>Box baling:</strong> place two lengths of sisal twine in the grooves of an open-ended wooden box; fill it with layers of dry hay, pressing each layer down with feet or a board; tie the twine tightly over the top; lift the box off the bale. <strong>Stacking:</strong> build a base of poles or stones, pile hay in layers, keep the centre firm, shape the top into a cone and thatch it. <strong>Storage:</strong> a dry, well-ventilated, raised shed protected from rain, rodents and fire. <strong>Qualities of good hay:</strong> leafy, greenish colour, sweet smell, soft, dry, free of mould, soil and weeds. Mouldy hay can make animals sick and must not be fed.</p>
    </div>
