    <div style="background:#10233d;padding:20px;border-radius:14px;margin-bottom:20px;border:1px solid #33415c;border-left:4px solid #22d3ee;">
    <h3 style="color:#c8e6c9;margin-top:0;">🔭 Business Background</h3>
    <p><strong>Effects of drought on livestock:</strong> shortage of feed and water, weight loss, low milk yield, disease, death and forced cheap sales. <strong>How households can adopt forage conservation:</strong> (1) <em>plan a feed calendar</em> — know which months have surplus and which have shortage; (2) <em>make a feed budget</em> — estimate how much feed the animals need during the dry months; (3) <em>conserve surplus</em> by stacking, baling or leaving standing forage; (4) <em>use crop residues</em> such as maize stover and bean haulms instead of burning them; (5) <em>plant fodder</em> such as Napier grass, Rhodes grass and fodder trees (for example calliandra and leucaena) on boundaries; (6) <em>store hay properly</em>; (7) <em>ration feed</em> — feed measured amounts and give priority to milking and young animals; (8) <em>reduce herd size</em> before drought by selling weaker animals while prices are good; (9) <em>work in groups</em> — share balers, build community hay stores and buy or sell hay together; (10) <em>follow early warning</em> messages on weather from radio and extension officers. Hay conservation also links to coping with climate change, which is making droughts more frequent.</p>
    </div>
